5. BASELINE — DEFAULT TLV BEHAVIOUR  [reference measurement]
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Purpose: establish what the DUT transmits BEFORE any tlv-select configuration.

  show lldp interface port1.0.23   (before change)
   Port     Rx/Tx  Notif  Management Addr  Base       802.1    802.3    MED
   1.0.23   Rx Tx  -- --  10.38.215.69     ---------- -------- -------- McNpLoPe--

  Captured frames on eth3 (30 s interval, matching 'Tx Timer Interval 30 secs'):
   15:04:55.369456  LLDP, length 50
   15:05:25.668862  LLDP, length 50

  Decode of frame 1:
   Ethernet Src 86:e3:27:87:09:c0 -> Dst 01:80:c2:00:00:0e (LLDP_Multicast)
   TLV 1   Chassis Id     : MAC address 84:e3:27:87:09:c0
   TLV 2   Port Id        : Interface name "port1.0.23"
   TLV 3   Time To Live   : 120 sec
   TLV 127 Org Specific   : OUI e0:1a:ea (Allied Telesis), subtype 2, len 20,
                            payload 2d561ddfbd8f4cd69f9c94b9c51dc64b  [proprietary]
   TLV 0   End of LLDPDU

CONTRAST WITH 'show lldp local-info interface port1.0.23', which at the same moment
reported Port Description, System Name, System Description, System Capabilities,
Management Address, Port VLAN ID, Port & Protocol VLAN, VLAN Names, Protocol IDs,
MAC/PHY Auto-negotiation and Link Aggregation as present.

  RESULT: Only the four mandatory TLVs (1,2,3,0) plus one Allied Telesis proprietary
          TLV are transmitted by default. NONE of the optional TLVs reach the wire.
          The customer's per-port 'lldp tlv-select all' is therefore load-bearing —
          without it, an LLDP-based discovery/NMS sees chassis, port and TTL only.

