N3 — WITHDRAW ONE TLV FROM A FULL SET
    Config   : interface port1.0.23 / lldp tlv-select all
                                    / no lldp tlv-select vlan-names
    Expected : VLAN Name TLVs removed; all other TLVs, incl. Port VLAN ID, retained.
    Window   : 15:18:53.2 - 15:19:21.7   (epoch 1786331933.179 - 1786331961.683)

    show lldp interface port1.0.23
     1.0.23   Rx Tx  -- --  10.38.215.69     PdSnSdScMa PvPp--Pi MpPoLaMf McNpLoPe--
                                                            ^^ Vn cleared, Pv/Pp/Pi intact

    Captured frames:
     15:18:40.5  len 561  total_tlv=37  org127=28  vlan_names=default,VLAN0072,
                                        VLAN0074,VLAN0076,VLAN0078,VLAN0100,VLAN_MGMT
     15:18:44.5  len 442  total_tlv=30  org127=21  vlan_names=(none)
     15:18:54.6  len 442  total_tlv=30  org127=21  vlan_names=(none)
     15:19:04.7  len 442  total_tlv=30  org127=21  vlan_names=(none)
     15:19:14.8  len 442  total_tlv=30  org127=21  vlan_names=(none)

    Arithmetic check:
      org TLV count  28 - 21 = 7 removed  == 7 VLAN Name TLVs previously present.
      frame length  561 - 442 = 119 octets removed.
      Predicted     7 x (2 hdr + 3 OUI + 1 subtype + 2 VID + 1 len) = 63 octets
                    + name payload (7+8+8+8+8+8+9) = 56 octets
                    = 119 octets.  MATCHES EXACTLY.

    RESULT: PASS. Withdrawal is surgical — precisely the 7 VLAN Name TLVs were
            removed, byte-for-byte, with all base, remaining 802.1 and all 802.3
            TLVs left intact. Port VLAN ID (PVID) survived, confirming that
            'vlan-names' and 'port-vlan' are independently controlled.

N4 — GLOBAL LLDP DISABLE
    Config   : configure terminal / no lldp run
    Expected : transmission ceases entirely.
    Window   : 15:19:31.3 - 15:19:59.8   (epoch 1786331971.291 - 1786331999.794)

    Captured frames:
     15:19:24.9  len  60  total_tlv=4  org127=0  nonorg=[1,2,3,0]  ttl=0
     (no further frames for the remainder of the capture)

    RESULT: PASS, with a correct-behaviour note. The DUT emitted exactly ONE final
            LLDPDU carrying Time To Live = 0 before going silent. This is the
            shutdown LLDPDU required by IEEE 802.1AB, which instructs neighbours to
            purge the entry immediately rather than wait out the hold time. Note the
            Allied Telesis proprietary TLV is absent from the shutdown frame
            (org127=0), so the shutdown LLDPDU is strictly mandatory TLVs only.
            Zero LLDPDUs were transmitted during the disabled window.

    NOTE: 'show lldp interface port1.0.23' still displayed the per-port tlv-select
          bitmap while LLDP was globally disabled. That is expected — the per-port
          selection is retained configuration, independent of the global run state —
          but it means the 'show' output alone cannot tell you whether the device is
          actually transmitting. Another instance of the section 3 rationale.

